PCTP Sec. 3, degree-of-freedom count for one wavepacket:
  physical: amplitude E0 (1) + carrier phase phi0 (1) + Poincare sphere (2) + OAM charge l (1, discrete) = 4 continuous + 1 discrete
  algebraic: q_a, q_i, q_j, q_k = 4 continuous reals
  q_a is assigned BOTH amplitude and phase (Eq. 6), so the map is not
  injective: 2 physical numbers collapse into 1 coordinate and cannot be
  read back out.  S1 of the Stokes vector is dropped entirely.

########## cqft.py ##########

=== CQFT Sec. 2.1: the stated obstruction ===
  j*i = -k  so (cA) (x) B with c=j, A=i, B=k gives -(k (x) k)
  j*k = i  so A (x) (cB)                 gives  (i (x) i)
  the paper's claim that bilinearity forces -(k(x)k) = i(x)i is ALGEBRAICALLY CORRECT.

=== CQFT Eq. (2): is |x| a tensor product? ===
  signature: H x H -> H, i.e. 4 + 4 real inputs -> 4 real outputs.
  a tensor product of two 4-dim spaces has dimension 16 (over R, as a
  bimodule over H it is still 4x4 = 16), so |x| cannot be one.
  six different input pairs reproducing the same product P, max error 1.67e-16
  the pre-image of any product is a 4-parameter family: 4 of the 8 input
  numbers are destroyed by every |x|, the operation is not invertible.

=== CQFT Eq. (4): the entangled state Psi_AB = (qA,t0) |x| (qB,t0) ===
  (1) |x| (i)      -> [0. 1. 0. 0.]
  (j) |x| (-k)     -> [ 0. -1.  0.  0.]
  two different two-particle configurations give the identical state; the
  formalism has no way to express correlation, hence none to express
  entanglement, which Sec. 3.2 is about.

=== CQFT Sec. 5: simulating the Causal Hysteresis Test ===
  the two paths give different joint states: ||p1 - p2|| = 0.415034
  photon A's reduced state differs:          ||rhoA1 - rhoA2|| = 0.000000
  photon B's reduced state:                  ||rhoB1 - rhoB2|| = 6.831e-18   <-- exactly zero
  worst ||rhoB1 - rhoB2|| over 20000 random non-commuting pairs: 4.605e-16
  no local operation on A, in any order, changes anything measurable on B
  alone.  A 'deterministic, measurable phase shift in Photon B' is a signal
  and would be faster-than-light communication.

  joint correlators E(a,b), which the paper says QM predicts to be equal:
    b = [1, 0, 0]:  path1 -0.963558   path2 -0.736970   difference -0.226588
    b = [0, 1, 0]:  path1 -0.172327   path2 -0.644218   difference +0.471890
    b = [0, 0, 1]:  path1 +0.204594   path2 +0.204594   difference +0.000000
  standard QM already predicts different correlations, so the experiment
  does not separate the two theories anywhere: where it could show a
  difference, QM predicts one too; where QM predicts none, CQFT's
  signature is forbidden by no-signalling.

=== does the causal index tau enter any computed quantity? ===
  distinct value components over 4 different (t1,t2) assignments: 1
  tau is not an argument of the value component.  Every prediction the
  three papers derive from |x| is a prediction of ordinary quaternion or
  octonion multiplication - Hamilton 1843, Graves 1843, Cayley 1845.
  What is added is a label on the operand order, which was already there.

